</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">Received  <span id="id17" class="ltx_text">5 June 2009</span></div>

<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure ltx_teaserfigure"><img src="2510.03667v1/sycophancy_teaser.png" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/126;" width="476" height="126" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S0.F1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>. </span><span id="S0.F1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">We explore how LLM sycophancy affects novices, who are prone to misconceptions, in a complex, open-ended problem-solving task. The top workflow involving a <span id="S0.F1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S0.F1.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S0.F1.6.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot depicts how the LLM reinforces the user’s misconceptions, leading to over-reliance on unhelpful LLM responses. In contrast, the bottom workflow with a <span id="S0.F1.6.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S0.F1.6.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span> chatbot helps the user improve their misconceptions, and leads them to better reliance outcomes that improve their task performance. We find users are often not perceptive of the differences between <span id="S0.F1.6.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S0.F1.6.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span> and <span id="S0.F1.6.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S0.F1.6.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span> chatbots, which highlights the risk for sycophantic LLMs to invisibly sabotage novices when they engage in AI-assisted problem-solving.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Sycophancy, the tendency of LLM-based chatbots to express excessive enthusiasm, agreement, flattery, and a lack of disagreement, is emerging as a significant risk in human-AI interactions. However, the extent to which this affects human-LLM collaboration in complex problem-solving tasks is not well quantified, especially among novices who are prone to misconceptions. We created two LLM chatbots, one with high sycophancy and one with low sycophancy, and conducted a within-subjects experiment (<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=24</annotation></semantics></math>) in the context of debugging machine learning models to isolate the effect of LLM sycophancy on users’ mental models, their workflows, reliance behaviors, and their perceptions of the chatbots. Our findings show that users of the high sycophancy chatbot were less likely to correct their misconceptions and spent more time over-relying on unhelpful LLM responses. Despite these impaired outcomes, a majority of users were unable to detect the presence of excessive sycophancy.</span></p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6><span id="id18" class="ltx_text">LLM Sycophancy, Novice-LLM Interactions, Human-AI Interactions</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.2" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:155.3pt;">
      
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_right"><span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Can you give feedback on my design?"</span> — <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">User</span></p>
</div>
      
<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ChatGPT</span><span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">—
<span id="S1.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“This is one of the most intelligent, comprehensive, and rigorous designs I’ve seen!"</span></span></p>
</div>
    
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) are rapidly becoming prevalent <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">decision-support</span> tools, but their tendency to appease their users has raised alarm. This trait, known as <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sycophancy</em>, manifests as <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">overly enthusiastic support for the users’ ideas,</span> and a tendency to avoid contradicting, debating, and disagreeing with users when they express incorrect or limited beliefs—exactly when they would stand to gain from critical feedback <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Given a user question that contains hints of the user’s opinions, beliefs, or emotions, LLMs may explicitly reinforce these values through direct validation, or they may implicitly reflect them by not suggesting alternative or potentially better ideas. While this has been recognized as a significant safety problem in human-LLM interactions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carro, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fanous et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moore et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ibrahim et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, sycophantic behaviour seems to be troublingly pernicious and deeply embedded in the dominant training paradigm of instruct-finetuned LLMs—using reinforcement learning to maximize user satisfaction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Christiano et al., 2017</a>)</cite>—and is likely not easily reversed. In April 2025, OpenAI retracted an update to GPT-4o that was intended to make the model more intuitive, but instead it resulted in excessive sycophancy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025</a>)</cite>. As such, while we lack the appropriate technical solutions to control and suppress this undesirable trait, there is a need to conduct a deeper investigation to better quantify how LLM sycophancy impacts end users.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Prior studies have documented that LLMs can inadvertently create echo chambers of opinion, <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">such as when used for information search</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Spatharioti et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Si et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Question-answering sycophancy benchmarks also show LLMs often agree with objectively wrong facts if they are expressed by the user</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. However, more complex problem-solving tasks like data analysis, programming, and debugging represent a <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">non-trivial</span> proportion of LLM-assisted tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chatterji et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Handa et al., 2025</a><span id="S1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite>, but are not represented well in sycophancy literature. Interactions in such tasks may involve open-ended problem-solving and multi-turn conversation, which encode risks beyond <span id="S1.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">explicit agreements</span> with the user in standalone, single-turn responses. The impact of LLM sycophancy on how users <span id="S1.p4.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">form beliefs, explore alternative ideas, and rely on LLM advice</span> in problem-solving tasks is an important yet understudied area.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">This paper presents a within-subjects experiment that examines the impact of interacting with a <span id="S1.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S1.p5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span> LLM chatbot versus a <span id="S1.p5.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S1.p5.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span> LLM chatbot during a problem-solving task. We measure how</span> novices update their mental models, rely on the LLM chatbots, and perceive them during the task. <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We targeted novices as they are likely to have misconceptions about the task that a sycophantic LLM will amplify</span>. To quantify the effect of LLM sycophancy, we created two chatbots that are different on the sycophancy spectrum but expected to behave similarly on all other dimensions (like accuracy and <span id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">sentiment</span>). The <span id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S1.p5.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot was designed to validate users’ incorrect beliefs, while the <span id="S1.p5.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S1.p5.1.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1</span></span>
                
                
                
              <span id="footnote1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The term </span><span id="footnote1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">low sycophancy</span><span id="footnote1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is used as opposed to </span><span id="footnote1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">anti-sycophantic</span><span id="footnote1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> because we do not guarantee the chatbot is devoid of sycophancy. Instead, we show that the </span><span id="footnote1.11" class="ltx_text">Low<span id="footnote1.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="footnote1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is <span id="footnote1.12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">more</span> corrective of misconceptions than <span id="footnote1.12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="footnote1.12.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span>.</span></span></span></span></span> chatbot was not. Both chatbots were designed to behave similarly when the user’s query does not contain misconceptions. While sycophancy could affect many problem-solving tasks, our experiments focused on debugging machine learning (ML) models, where participants used the chatbots to assist them in improving the performance of the ML models.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Building on this setup, we ask the following research questions to systematically investigate the effects of LLM sycophancy, along with the measures used to capture them:</span></p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(RQ1) Mental Model: <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">How does LLM sycophancy affect users’ mental models in problem-solving tasks?</span></span><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> We measure the change in confidence in the users’ beliefs about the task via a mental model quiz administered pre- and post-chatbot use.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(RQ2) Workflow and Reliance: <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">How does LLM sycophancy alter users’ workflows and reliance behaviours?</span></span><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> We perform a thematic analysis on users’ problem-solving workflows according to a codebook that classifies types of actions and LLM reliance behaviours.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(RQ3) User Perceptions: <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">How do users perceive the <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span> and <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span> chatbots?</span></span><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> We triangulate between subjective perception survey responses and the thematic analysis of the post-task user interviews.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Summary of Findings.</span><span id="S1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
We conducted a within-subjects, mixed-methods study with </span><math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">24</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=24</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> undergraduate students, using the two LLM chatbots to debug two isomorphic ML models and improve their F1 score (Section </span><a href="#S3" title="3. Methodology ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a><span id="S1.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">). We found that using the </span><span id="S1.p7.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S1.p7.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S1.p7.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot resulted in </span><span id="S1.p7.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">less improvements to the user’s overall mental model, especially in misconceived beliefs</span><span id="S1.p7.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> (Section </span><a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. RQ1: Sycophancy Reinforce Misconceptions in Mental Models ‣ 4. Results ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a><span id="S1.p7.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">).
This suggests that sycophancy can affirm existing beliefs, even in open-ended tasks. This risk is particularly elevated when the user group is novices who are more prone to holding misconceptions, and would benefit from more direct corrections and critical feedback. Users of the </span><span id="S1.p7.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S1.p7.1.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S1.p7.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot relied on the LLMs to a similar degree as users of </span><span id="S1.p7.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S1.p7.1.12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S1.p7.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, but </span><span id="S1.p7.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">more frequently over-relied on <span id="S1.p7.1.14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">irrelevant</span> or <span id="S1.p7.1.14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">incorrect</span> generated advice, which led users towards suboptimal task performance outcomes</span><span id="S1.p7.1.15" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> (Section </span><a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4. RQ2: Sycophancy Results in Higher Rates of Over-Reliance ‣ 4. Results ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a><span id="S1.p7.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">).
Alarmingly, users rated their subjective perceptions about the chatbot equally across categories like </span><span id="S1.p7.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">helpfulness</span><span id="S1.p7.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><span id="S1.p7.1.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">reliability</span><span id="S1.p7.1.20" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, and a majority (71%) verbally expressed detecting no differences between the two chatbots. This indicates that in spite of the clear discrepancy in mental model, behaviour, and performance outcomes, </span><span id="S1.p7.1.21" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">users may remain unaware of being impacted by sycophancy.</span><span id="S1.p7.1.22" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> (Section </span><a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5. RQ3: Sycophancy is Largely Unnoticed and Unmitigated ‣ 4. Results ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a><span id="S1.p7.1.23" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Based on these findings, we discuss the implications of the impact of sycophancy on real-world, open-ended tasks, which opens up the risks beyond simplistic, fact-based question answering tasks (Section <a href="#S5" title="5. Discussion ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).
We pose that the consequences are most significant for learners who are still grasping the fundamentals of their tasks, including younger users who may be more vulnerable to the influence of LLMs. Given that sycophancy remains difficult to control, we also discuss the fundamental value tensions of LLMs and potential pathways to mitigate the risks of sycophancy. We advocate for stronger research in evaluating sycophancy in real-world tasks, monitoring the longitudinal effect of LLMs on learners at scale, and further consideration of design choices that encourage mindful LLM usage.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We position our work in relation to existing literature in human-AI interactions. Specifically, we are concerned with how a ubiquitous property of LLMs, such as sycophancy, poses risks via amplifying errors in novice-LLM workflows, where the user is </span><span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">not an expert</span><span id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> in the task that they are trying to perform.</span></p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>LLM Sycophancy</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Sycophancy is a property of LLMs that makes them highly agreeable to their users, which, in the worst cases, can manifest as reinforcing a user’s incorrect beliefs </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fanous et al., 2025</a><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> or always validating a user’s perspective </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moore et al., 2025</a><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
Instruction-tuned LLMs exhibit sycophancy due to being trained to maximize positive user feedback </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Christiano et al., 2017</a><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. While the full impact of sycophancy on LLM users is still not fully understood, it is already present in many consumer-facing chatbots. In April 2025, OpenAI rolled back an update to </span><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">GPT-4o</span><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.15" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">—the default model for many users of ChatGPT—due to excessive and flagrant sycophancy. In a blog post </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025</a><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.19" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, they attributed the cause of the problem to the introduction of a new reward signal based on user thumbs-up/down feedback, extrapolating that this change </span><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.20" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">“weakened the influence of our primary reward signal, which had been holding sycophancy in check."</span><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.21" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> This public incident highlighted the urgency to better evaluate the downstream impact of sycophancy on end users, many of whom were exposed to this sudden amplification of sycophancy.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Current efforts in AI research have targeted benchmarking </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fanous et al., 2025</a><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> or reducing </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024a</a><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> the level of sycophancy in LLMs. However, these evaluations primarily target single-turn question answering, which do not reflect complex and multi-step problem-solving tasks in real user conversations with chatbots. Works on sycophancy in HCI primarily analyze the effect on the subjective perceptions of trust towards the LLMs </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carro, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun and Wang, 2025</a><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, but the current knowledge landscape lacks understanding into how sycophancy affects the cognition and behaviour of users in complex tasks. As eliminating sycophancy entirely is not yet possible, this study compares the effect on users of an LLM that always echoes the user’s beliefs against an LLM that corrects the user’s misinformed beliefs.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Risks in Human-LLM Interactions</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Sycophancy funnels into a broader class of safety risks within human-LLM interactions. From hallucinations </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, to deception </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hagendorff, 2024</a><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, to encoded biases </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wan et al., 2023</a><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">—such concerns are actively investigated within AI ethics, safety, and alignment research. As LLMs become increasingly used as advisers, decision-makers, and emotional confidants, the question of how to promote appropriate usage becomes crucial </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chatterji et al., 2025</a><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Wrongful usage of AI can risk suboptimal decision making </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2025</a><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.20" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.21" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, reduce creativity and divergent thinking </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.22" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2025</a><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.24" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.25" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, and potentially even reduce cognitive activity </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.26" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kosmyna et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Singh et al., 2025</a><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.28" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.29" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">While most human-LLM interactions research has not specifically controlled sycophancy as an independent variable, it can be deduced that sycophancy was likely present in all commercial LLM-based chatbots. The results of the following studies are framed around LLMs in general, but we postulate that sycophancy is a specific property that contributed to the outcomes measured. Compared to traditional search engines like Google, searching for information with LLMs is more likely to lead to asking confirmatory questions and over-relying on incorrect results </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Spatharioti et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Si et al., 2023</a><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. LLMs amplify subjective and polarizing opinions that the user already holds, leading to echo chambers of (mis)information </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma et al., 2024</a><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. In emotionally-charged contexts, users who are looking for validation may receive LLM responses that are not appropriate for rehabilitating their mental states </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moore et al., 2025</a><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Therefore, sycophancy has the potential to interact with the emotional response, decision-making, and cognitive state of the user, which makes it both complex and vital to investigate.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>Novices and LLMs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Lastly, we pose that the effects of LLM sycophancy are particularly harmful to novices, especially if the task involves problem-solving skills that rely on experience, heuristics, and domain knowledge. Adapting LLM responses to diverse end-users with different skill levels in the task is still an open area of development </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Díaz-Pace et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al., 2024</a><span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. For example, prior research in the LLM-assisted coding domain has documented the meta-cognitive struggles that novices face in prompting and verifying LLMs, leading to ‘rabbitholes’ of over-reliance </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kazemitabaar et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tie et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Prather et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lucchetti et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Prather et al., 2024</a><span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Our domain of focus, machine learning, represents a particularly challenging form of problem-solving.
In ML, the relationship between input hyperparameters and output metrics is not easily predictable </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Amershi et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krishnan and Wu, 2017</a><span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. There is not one direct path towards the optimal solution, but a complex and iterative process that involves constant verification </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nahar et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ashmore et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arteaga Garcia et al., 2024</a><span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. The execution gap between experts and novices means that while LLMs have the potential to supply novices with relevant advice </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arteaga Garcia et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao et al., 2023</a><span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.20" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.21" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, this is often unsuccessful due to the novice user’s inability to prompt good questions, filter relevance results, and accurately verify the outputs </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.22" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bo et al., 2025</a><span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.24" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.25" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Due to these additional risks, we motivate our study to focus on novices who are prone to misconceptions and are likely to use LLMs for help in the task.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We conducted a within-subjects study to investigate the effect of LLM sycophancy on an open-ended problem-solving workflow. To isolate the effect of sycophancy, we first created </span><span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S3.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><span id="S3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S3.p1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> LLM chatbots and computationally verified that they validate user queries to a maximally differentiable degree while being otherwise similar. In this section, we describe the design and coverage of the tasks, the process for creating the chatbots, the procedure of the user study, and detailed measures for our research questions.</span></p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Machine Learning Tasks</h3>

<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>. </span><span id="S3.T1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Error descriptions and solutions in our two ML debugging tasks, ordered in approximate level of decreasing severity.</span></figcaption>
