<span id="S3.T1.7.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.7.3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Severe data imbalance at a 10:1 ratio.</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.7.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.3.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:112.4pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.3.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.7.3.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Set </span><span id="S3.T1.7.3.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">class_weight</span><span id="S3.T1.7.3.3.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">=</span><span id="S3.T1.7.3.3.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">balanced</span><span id="S3.T1.7.3.3.1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, or resample data.</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.7.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.3.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:11.4pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.3.4.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.7.3.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">LR1</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.7.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.3.5.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:105.3pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.3.5.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.7.3.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Lack of feature scaling in a distance-sensitive LR model.</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.7.3.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.3.6.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:93.9pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.3.6.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.7.3.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Apply </span><span id="S3.T1.7.3.6.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Standard Scaler</span><span id="S3.T1.7.3.6.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> in the data processing step.</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.7.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.7.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.4.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:11.4pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.7.4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">RF2</span></span>
</span></th>
<td id="S3.T1.7.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.4.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:99.6pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.7.4.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Overfitting of the RF model due to lack of complexity hyperparameters.</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.7.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.4.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:112.4pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.4.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.7.4.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Limit model complexity, such as </span><span id="S3.T1.7.4.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">max_depth=5</span><span id="S3.T1.7.4.3.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.7.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.4.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:11.4pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.4.4.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.7.4.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">LR2</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.7.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.4.5.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:105.3pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.4.5.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.7.4.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Underfitting of the LR model due to over-regularization.</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.7.4.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.4.6.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:93.9pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.4.6.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.7.4.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Tune hyperparameter </span><span id="S3.T1.7.4.6.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">C = 100</span><span id="S3.T1.7.4.6.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.7.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.7.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.5.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:11.4pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.7.5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">RF3</span></span>
</span></th>
<td id="S3.T1.7.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.5.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:99.6pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.5.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.7.5.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Dataset is not shuffled when split.</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.7.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.5.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:112.4pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.5.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.7.5.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Set </span><span id="S3.T1.7.5.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">shuffle=True</span><span id="S3.T1.7.5.3.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> in </span><span id="S3.T1.7.5.3.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">train_test_split().</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.7.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.5.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:11.4pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.5.4.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.7.5.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">LR3</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.7.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.5.5.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:105.3pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.5.5.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.7.5.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Mild data imbalance at a 55:45 ratio.</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.7.5.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.5.6.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:93.9pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.5.6.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.7.5.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Set </span><span id="S3.T1.7.5.6.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">class_weight = balanced</span><span id="S3.T1.7.5.6.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.7.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.7.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.6.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:11.4pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.7.6.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">RF4</span></span>
</span></th>
<td id="S3.T1.7.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.6.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:99.6pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.6.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.7.6.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Categorical data is not correctly processed.</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.7.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.6.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:112.4pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.6.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.7.6.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Apply one-hot encoding to categorical features.</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.7.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.6.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:11.4pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.6.4.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.7.6.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">LR4</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.7.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.6.5.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:105.3pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.6.5.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.7.6.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Outliers (</span><math id="S3.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{z-score}&gt;10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">z-score</mtext><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&gt;</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{z-score}&gt;10</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.T1.7.6.5.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) in training dataset.</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.7.6.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.6.6.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:93.9pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.6.6.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.7.6.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Use </span><span id="S3.T1.7.6.6.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">z-score</span><span id="S3.T1.7.6.6.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> to detect and drop the outlier samples.</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We center our experiment on debugging machine learning models as the task, and self-identified ML novices as the participants. Novices refer to learners in the task who are familiar with basic concepts of ML (such as knowing the purpose of training and testing datasets, reading model performance metrics, and describing failure modes like over-fitting and under-fitting), but may not be fluent or experienced in all aspects of ML theory or implementation. We developed two scripts for training binary classification ML models using concepts familiar to an introductory audience: </span><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Random Forest</span><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> (</span><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">RF</span><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) with the Adult Income prediction task </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Becker and Kohavi, 1996</a><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Logistic Regression</span><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> (</span><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">LR</span><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) with the Wine Quality prediction task </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cortez and Reis, 2009</a><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. We further modified the datasets to support the goal of creating ‘debuggable’ training code, such as changing the magnitudes of the features in Wine Quality to induce feature scaling issues and dropping some features to reduce the complexity of the Adult Income dataset.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Each task was intentionally planted with four conceptual errors that affect the performance of the models. The errors were purposely screened and selected such that they differ between the two tasks to reduce potential learning effects in a within-subjects setting. Whenever possible, we strived to ensure that each error contributes </span><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">distinct</span><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> deterioration in performance. See Table </span><a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.1. Machine Learning Tasks ‣ 3. Methodology ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> for the description of the errors and the proposed solutions. Appendix </span><a href="#A1" title="Appendix A ML Debugging Tasks ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> further shows the full assignment code and the proposed code fixes. Note that there can be multiple ways to solve an error, such as applying </span><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">class_weights=‘balanced’</span><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> in the model parameters or resampling the data with the SMOTE algorithm </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chawla et al., 2002</a><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> to fix data imbalance issues. We would consider either solution to be conceptually correct.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">For each debugging task in the study, participants were given a description explaining the context of why the model is trained. For example, we motivated the </span><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">RF</span><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> task as </span><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">‘Your goal is to identify all of the high-income citizens, without mistakenly identifying any low-income citizens’</span><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
To introduce some natural ambiguity, we did not explicitly say which metric out of the ones provided (accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, and confusion matrix of both the training and testing datasets) they should optimize.
Given that the </span><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">RF</span><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> task’s Adult Income dataset is highly imbalanced, this implies that the appropriate metric to optimize is the F1-score of the </span><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">high-earning</span><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> class, rather than the overall accuracy. See Figure </span><a href="#A1.F1" title="Figure A.1 ‣ Appendix A ML Debugging Tasks ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> of the Appendix to see the custom </span><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">get_performance()</span><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> function provided to the participants.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We further verified these two tasks through multiple pilot studies with ML learners and practitioners of various levels who were unaffiliated with the research team and who were further excluded from the main participant pool. In the pilots, we made improvements to the comprehensibility of the task instructions and checked that the conceptual issues and solutions were of an appropriate skill level for novices.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Creating <span id="S3.SS2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S3.SS2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span> and <span id="S3.SS2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S3.SS2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span> Chatbots</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In order to measure the effects of LLM sycophancy, we designed two chatbots that differ in their sycophancy towards users, but are otherwise similar. Before deploying the chatbots in our experiment, we conducted computational evaluations to validate that the </span><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot conditions behave as expected.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Chatbot Behaviour Goals.</span><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
 
We define a sycophantic LLM as one that reflects and supports the user’s mental model of the task, irrespective of the correctness of their hypotheses and beliefs. To study the effects of sycophancy, we seek to </span><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">emphasize the difference in model response behavior when the user has the wrong mental model.</span><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> Specifically, we define the following goals:</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I1.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(D1)</span></span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Maximally differentiated validation characteristics when the user query contains a misconception — high validation of the user’s beliefs for </span><span id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and low validation for </span><span id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I1.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(D2)</span></span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Similar performance in the ML task when the user query does </span><span id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">not</span><span id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> contain any misconceptions.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I1.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(D3)</span></span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Similar sentiments in the tone of the response.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">It should be noted that the design goal </span><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(D1)</span><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> necessitates revealing the task solutions to the chatbot system, which were provided to help the LLM infer if the user’s beliefs were misconceived or not</span><span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            <span id="footnote2.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">This design choice makes the LLMs more similar to an “oracle” AI chatbot rather than a generic AI chatbot, which would not always have the relevant task context. This is appropriate, as oracle AI chatbots (e.g. those with access to homework answers) are used in educational contexts.</span></span></span></span><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> The following section describes how we developed the system prompts for the </span><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> LLMs so that both had access to detected user misconceptions, and instructed the </span><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot to amplify detected misconceptions.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Prompt Engineering and System Design.</span><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
To enable the desired behaviour described above, we introduce an intermediate LLM that is responsible for </span><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">inferring the user’s beliefs and misconceptions</span><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. The output of the </span><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Misconception Inference LLM</span><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is then passed, along with the prior conversation and the task code, to one of two output models: the </span><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> or the </span><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot. For convenience, we collectively refer to these two models as the </span><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Answering LLM</span><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
This full system diagram, including the </span><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Misconception Inference LLM</span><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and the </span><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Answering LLM</span><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, is shown in Figure </span><a href="#A2.F1" title="Figure B.1 ‣ Appendix B Chatbot System Prompts ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
The </span><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Answering LLM</span><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.19" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> does not actually receive the full task solutions; it only receives the user’s misconceptions as identified in the prior step, but this may contain allusions to the solution.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The misconception inference step also naturally encourages the underlying </span><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">GPT-4.1</span><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> model to correct the identified misconceptions without being explicitly prompted to do so. Thus, the only difference between </span><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is that </span><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is encouraged to be sycophantic in its behavior through system prompting. </span><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> does not receive any additional instructions,
which reduces the sources of variations in our conditions and improves the ecological validity of the chatbots.
See Appendix </span><a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Additional Computational Evaluation Results ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> for the system prompts used for the </span><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Misconception Inference LLM</span><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and both the </span><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.17.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> Answering LLMs.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Computational Evaluation.</span><span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
To benchmark the chatbots, we compiled and adapted real user queries asked in the two ML tasks from previous pilot studies. These computational evaluations are not comprehensive and should be interpreted only in the context of our ML debugging tasks. Due to the system prompts encoding task-specific instructions, it was not possible to conduct generalized benchmark evaluations.
We compare the two conditions across the previously specified characteristics and report the results:</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Differentiated Validation Behaviour</span><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">: We aim to achieve maximally different validation behaviour of the LLM when the user has misconceptions. To measure this, we sampled 15 user queries from each ML task that contained a misconception (30 total) and prompted each chatbot to respond. For evaluation, we used an </span><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">LLM-as-Judge</span><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> evaluation set-up with </span><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">GPT-4o</span><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, which was presented with randomized chatbot responses in a blinded setup (i.e., without knowledge of which chatbot generated which response). This enabled us to assess the pairwise win rate of which chatbot </span><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">validates the user’s perspective</span><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> more. Averaged across </span><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=3</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> judging trials per query, </span><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.15" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> was rated to be more validating 75.6% of the time, </span><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> 7.8% of the time, and the conditions were equal 16.6% of the time. Using a binomial test and dropping the equal predictions, the winrate of </span><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.18.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.19" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> was significantly higher than chance (</span><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.0001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&lt;</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">.0001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.0001</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.20" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">).</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Equal Performance in Task</span><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">: To understand if the system prompt may have impacted LLM’s performance — and therefore the quality of the advice they offer — we collected 15 open-ended and conceptual user queries from each task (again 30 total). In the same format, the LLM chatbots were prompted and their answers were judged in a blinded, pairwise fashion. Averaged across </span><math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=3</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> judging trials per query, </span><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> was rated to be more accurate 23.3% of the time, </span><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> 35.6% of the time, and the conditions were equal 41.1% of the time. While a slight difference between conditions was observed, a binomial test shows the difference was not statistically significant (</span><math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.16</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">).</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Equal Sentiment</span><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">: Lastly, sentiment can often be seen as an indication of sycophancy. We therefore aimed to evaluate whether sentiment differences existed between the two chatbots.
We used the pre-trained sentiment classifier TweetEval </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barbieri et al., 2020</a><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> to predict the probability of </span><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">positive</span><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">neutral</span><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, and </span><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">negative</span><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> sentiments in the tone of the chatbot’s response. We evaluated sentiment on both datasets of </span><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">misconceived</span><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">open-ended</span><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> queries from the two previous evaluations. We find that </span><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.17.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">’s predicted negative sentiment was only significantly higher than </span><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.19.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.20" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> by the Mann-Whitney U-test (</span><math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.0001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&lt;</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">.0001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.0001</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.21" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) </span><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.22" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">when responding to misconceptions</span><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.23" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. There was no significant difference when the questions were open-ended and conceptual, which is desirable. The graphical results can be found in Figure </span><a href="#A3.F1" title="Figure C.1 ‣ Appendix C Additional Computational Evaluation Results ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.24" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> in the Appendix.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">To summarize the computational tests, we confirmed that our two conditions achieve significantly different validation behaviour when asked misconceived questions, but do not differ significantly in performance or tone when asked neutral and open-ended questions. Therefore, the conditions exhibited satisfactory performance for our main experiments in the context of our ML debugging tasks.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>RQs and Measures</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We describe the three research questions and their measures, originally summarized in Section </span><a href="#S1" title="1. Introduction ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a><span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, in detail here.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">RQ1: Mental Models.</span><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
The first objective centers around measuring whether excessive sycophancy affects users’ mental models of the tasks. We operationalize as the change in the confidence of their beliefs about the task. The literature around measuring mental models is not very standardized, with many studies relying on capturing qualitative themes from interview data </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thompson et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. While this approach captures appropriately rich descriptions and individual differences, it does not support quantitative analyses of </span><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">changes</span><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> in mental models. Instead, we follow the style of prior research that uses standardized quiz questions to measure knowledge in the task </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nourani et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Collaris et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kulesza et al., 2013</a><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. We further aim to discriminate between </span><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">strong</span><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">weak</span><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> beliefs, which helps quantify if interacting with the chatbots affects the </span><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">strength</span><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> of users’ beliefs.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Based on these requirements, we developed a quiz comprised of 12 true or false hypotheses about the task — for example, ‘random state </span><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">does not significantly affect model performance’</span><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">‘The data loading code contains a misstep’</span><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. The statements were generated using the beliefs and misconceptions captured from pilot study participants as a guideline. Both tasks shared the same set of statements. For each task, 6/12 statements were correct and 6/12 were incorrect, and only 2/6 of the correct statements and 2/6 of the incorrect statements overlapped between the two tasks. During the study, participants rated their confidence in each statement with a slider spanning -100 to 100, where -100 is a confident belief that is is </span><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">false</span><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, 0 is </span><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">unsure</span><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, and 100 is a confident belief that it is </span><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">true</span><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. See Appendix </span><a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Mental Model Quiz ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> for the statements in the mental model quiz and how they apply to each ML task.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">To understand the impact of sycophancy on participants’ mental models in the debugging task, we evaluated the changes in their confidence ratings in the </span><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Mental Model Quiz</span><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> pre- and post-chatbot use.
While each statement in the quiz could be either true or false, we normalized the directions such that a </span><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">positive confidence</span><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> in a statement is always a correct belief, while a </span><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">negative confidence</span><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is always a wrong belief. Accordingly, we can compute the following metrics: </span><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(A)</span><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> confidence-weighted accuracy, which describes the mean accuracy of their beliefs weighted by their self-rated confidence; and </span><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(B)</span><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> count-based accuracy, which only accounts for the number of correct beliefs.
In the following equations,  </span><math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is the number of participants, </span><math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is the number of questions, and confidence can be either pre- or post-chatbot (the pre-post difference is be computed as the change):</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I3.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I3.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(A)</span></span> 
<div id="S3.I3.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Confidence-Weighted Acc. = </span><math id="S3.I3.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{P}\sum_{p=1}^{P}\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\text{confidence}_{p,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∑</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi></msubsup><mrow><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∑</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></msubsup><msub><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">confidence</mtext><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{P}\sum_{p=1}^{P}\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\text{confidence}_{p,n}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I3.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(B)</span></span> 
<div id="S3.I3.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Count-Based Acc. = </span><math id="S3.I3.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{P}\sum_{p=1}^{P}\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\mathbf{1}\!\left(\text{confidence}_{p,n}&gt;0\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∑</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi></msubsup><mrow><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">confidence</mtext><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&gt;</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{P}\sum_{p=1}^{P}\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\mathbf{1}\!\left(\text{confidence}_{p,n}&gt;0\right)</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">RQ2: Workflows and Reliance.</span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
To understand users’ behaviour and reliance on the LLM, we thematically analyzed the workflows of participants in each task using a codebook approach. This is a standard approach in analyzing participant actions and synthesizing findings on LLM interaction patterns </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yen et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023b</a><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
The types of event in our user workflows are: </span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">User</span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Query</span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Chatbot</span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Response</span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, and </span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Code</span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Change</span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. We assigned each event with two labels — the </span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">detailed action</span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.20" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, which breaks down the event into further sub-categories, and the </span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.21" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">outcome</span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.22" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, which describes the correctness or relevance of the action. For example, a </span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.23" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Code</span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.24" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.25" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Change</span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.26" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> action can involve a </span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.27" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">minor change</span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.28" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> that </span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.29" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">improves</span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.30" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> the performance, and a </span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.31" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Chatbot</span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.32" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.33" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Response</span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.34" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> can be </span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.35" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">corrective</span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.36" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and provide </span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.37" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">helpful</span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.38" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> advice. </span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.39" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">User</span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.40" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.41" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Query</span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.42" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is only labelled with the </span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.43" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">detailed action</span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.44" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, for example, inserting a </span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.45" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">misconceived lead</span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.46" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> into their question (like asking how to perform feature scaling in the </span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.47" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">RF</span><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.48" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> task, which is unnecessary).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We then parsed the event log into coherent workflow </span><span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">chunks</span><span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, defined as the sequence of events encompassing a distinct and specific goal — for example, iterating on the implementation of a specific feature, or brainstorming on a specific topic.
Since our end goal was to quantify whether the user relied on the LLM or not, and whether the user’s reliance decision was helpful or not, we used the events within a workflow chunk to make a classification on reliance over the workflow chunk.
For each defined workflow chunk, we analyzed the key actions taken and categorize the chunk into one of the five reliance outcome classes. The reliance patterns are defined through adapting prior work on appropriate AI reliance </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schemmer et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bo et al., 2024</a><span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">:</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Over-Reliance:</span><span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> Indicated by an </span><span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">unhelpful</span><span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> misconceived query, a </span><span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">confirmatory</span><span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> response, and an </span><span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">inappropriate</span><span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> reliance action (such as applying conceptually wrong code)</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Under-Reliance:</span><span id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> Indicated by a </span><span id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">helpful</span><span id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot reply, but the reliance action is </span><span id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">ignoring</span><span id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> the suggested actions.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Appropriate Reliance on LLM:</span><span id="S3.I4.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> Indicated by a </span><span id="S3.I4.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">helpful</span><span id="S3.I4.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot reply with an </span><span id="S3.I4.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">appropriate</span><span id="S3.I4.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> reliance action (such as applying conceptually correct code).</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Appropriate Reliance on Self:</span><span id="S3.I4.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> Indicated by a </span><span id="S3.I4.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">confirmatory</span><span id="S3.I4.i4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> reply to an </span><span id="S3.I4.i4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">unhelpful</span><span id="S3.I4.i4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> query, but the user correctly </span><span id="S3.I4.i4.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">ignores</span><span id="S3.I4.i4.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> the otherwise harmful advice.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(5)</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Conceptual:</span><span id="S3.I4.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> Indicated by the user asking a conceptual or definition query to improve their understanding, without resulting in any code changes.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The codebook for the events and reliance chunks was developed through the research team’s iterative discussions on initial pilot study results. After collecting the main experiment results, two researchers who were involved in the data collection then independently annotated three participant workflows and assessed inter-rater reliability (IRR). Using Cohen’s kappa as the agreement metric, the annotators revised the definition of the codes until the agreement rate reached a high value (</span><math id="S3.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\geq 0.60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">κ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">≥</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.60</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\geq 0.60</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS3.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">). The finalized codebook was then applied to deductively code all participants’ task workflows. See the full codebook in Table </span><a href="#A6.T2" title="Table F.2 ‣ Appendix F Workflow Codebook ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.2</span></a><span id="S3.SS3.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> in Appendix </span><a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Workflow Codebook ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a><span id="S3.SS3.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">RQ3: Subjective Perceptions.</span><span id="S3.SS3.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
Lastly, we captured participants’ self-reported perceptions of their interactions with the chatbot, including the effect of the chatbot on their mental models, learning, and engagement in the task. We used a mixed-methods approach by combining a Likert survey after using the chatbots, think-aloud while completing the surveys, and an additional semi-structured interview after the conclusion of both tasks. In the interview, we asked participants to contrast the experiences they had with the two chatbots in the experiment. We also inquired into their strategies and feelings on using chatbots in real life, with an emphasis on learning and education scenarios. The </span><span id="S3.SS3.p8.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Subjective Perceptions Survey</span><span id="S3.SS3.p8.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> consists of the following 7-point Likert statements, divided into three broad categories of impact:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Impact on Self:</span></p>
<ul id="S3.I5" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(Chatbot’s Effect on Confidence in Ideas)</span><span id="S3.I5.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> “The chatbot made me more confident in my ideas."</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(Chatbot’s Effect on Diversifying Ideas)</span><span id="S3.I5.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> “The chatbot helped me consider alternative ideas."</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I5.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(Self-Learning)</span><span id="S3.I5.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> “I have learned skills necessary for solving this type of problem in the future."</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Impact on Usage Experience:</span></p>
<ul id="S3.I6" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I6.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(Chatbot Helpfulness)</span><span id="S3.I6.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> “The chatbot is helpful in the task."</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I6.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(Chatbot Reliability)</span><span id="S3.I6.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> “I can rely on the chatbot."</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I6.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I6.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(Enjoyment of Chatbot)</span><span id="S3.I6.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> “I enjoyed using the chatbot."</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Impact on Task:</span></p>
<ul id="S3.I7" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I7.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I7.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I7.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I7.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(Improvement in Task)</span><span id="S3.I7.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> “I believe the ML model was significantly improved after debugging."</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I7.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I7.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I7.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I7.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(Engagement in Task</span><span id="S3.I7.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) “I was cognitively engaged in the task."</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The think-aloud and interview transcripts were thematically analyzed </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S3.SS3.p12.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clarke and Braun, 2014</a><span id="S3.SS3.p12.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S3.SS3.p12.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
While we did not employ a strictly structured deductive approach, we shaped the qualitative analysis by focusing on themes that related to a) subjective perceptions of the two chatbots, b) identification of problematic chatbot characteristics in real-world usage, and c) strategies around working with sycophantic chatbots. These topics were related to our research question and have been defined based on pilot study findings.
We grouped recurring codes into broader themes and triangulated them with the survey results to describe richer findings and strengthen the interpretation of the results. The research team discussed their interpretation of the interview results and negotiated disagreements until resolution.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S3.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><img src="2510.03667v1/user_study_procedure_v2.png" id="S3.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/185;" width="476" height="185" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.sf1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S3.F2.sf1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Within-subjects user study procedure.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S3.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><img src="2510.03667v1/conversations_comparison_short_v2.png" id="S3.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/81;" width="476" height="81" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.sf2.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S3.F2.sf2.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Example of <span id="S3.F2.sf2.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S3.F2.sf2.10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span> and <span id="S3.F2.sf2.10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S3.F2.sf2.10.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span> responses to a misconceived question. </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>. </span><span id="S3.F2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Overview of the user study procedure and chatbot conditions evaluated.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>User Study</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We describe the recruitment and procedure of the user study.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Recruitment and Inclusion Criteria.</span><span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
We first conducted a power analysis to estimate the number of participants needed as 24.
The sample size was calculated using G*Power and based on </span><math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.05</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="power=0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">r</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">power=0.8</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, and a medium effect size of Cohen’s d </span><math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dz=0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">z</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dz=0.6</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, using the statistical test: </span><span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">‘Means: Difference between two dependent means (matched pairs)’</span><span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We targeted recruitment primarily from students who were currently enrolled, or had recently taken an undergraduate Introduction to Machine Learning course at our research institution.
Due to the limited class size, we also employed a number of other recruitment techniques, including snowball sampling from the existing participants, and posting the recruitment on various university communication channels, including class forums, Slack channels, and student clubs’ social media. To narrow in on the target population without introducing unnecessary friction in the registration process, we required participants to self-declare that they were </span><span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">“familiar with basic machine learning concepts but not an expert, such as having taken &lt;Introduction to ML&gt;, self-studied ML, or implemented small-scale ML projects"</span><span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. We did not impose any additional requirements on skill level. The experiment lasted a maximum of 90 minutes, and participants were compensated with a $25 CAD ($18 USD) gift card, which is in the range of the local minimum wage.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Experiment Procedure.</span><span id="S3.SS4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
The procedure of the experiment  is visualized in Figure </span><a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.3. RQs and Measures ‣ 3. Methodology ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a><span id="S3.SS4.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and described as follows:</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I8" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I8.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I8.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I8.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I8.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Participants filled out the intake form, which included a consent form and asked about basic demographics information. They also took a </span><span id="S3.I8.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Knowledge Screening Quiz</span><span id="S3.I8.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> consisting of five multiple-choice questions (see Appendix </span><a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Knowledge Screening Quiz ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a><span id="S3.I8.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">), where they selected both the answer and their confidence in their answer. This provided us with information about their baseline theoretical knowledge in machine learning.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I8.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I8.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I8.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I8.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Participants were provided with information about the study and a walkthrough of the experiment interface (a Google Colab notebook) using a warm-up task.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I8.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I8.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I8.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I8.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Participants were assigned to the first task-chatbot pairing, e.g. </span><span id="S3.I8.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">RF</span><span id="S3.I8.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">-</span><span id="S3.I8.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S3.I8.i3.p1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S3.I8.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, using the Latin square procedure for counter-balancing between conditions and tasks.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I8.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S3.I8.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I8.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I8.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Participants were provided time to explore the code and performance metrics, then they were instructed to write down their initial observations and hypotheses about potential errors present in the code. Forming hypotheses was intended to prime participants into ‘‘information-searching" mode with targeted goals, which would help them ask more structured and specific questions from the chatbot as opposed to offloading the cognitive load of the task to the chatbot</span><span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                    <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
                    
                    
                    
                  Through pilot studies, we found that open-ended, off-loading questions—such as <span id="footnote3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Tell me what’s wrong with the code”</span>—reveal little of the user’s beliefs. Consequently, the <span id="footnote3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="footnote3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="footnote3.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and <span id="footnote3.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="footnote3.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span> chatbots behave similarly, which dilutes the expected effect we intended to observe.</span></span></span></span><span id="S3.I8.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S3.I8.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bo et al., 2025</a><span id="S3.I8.i4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S3.I8.i4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I8.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(5)</span> 
<div id="S3.I8.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I8.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I8.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">After forming preliminary hypotheses, participants completed the </span><span id="S3.I8.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Initial Mental Model Quiz</span><span id="S3.I8.i5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> to record their current beliefs about the task.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I8.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(6)</span> 
<div id="S3.I8.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I8.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I8.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Participants were given 10-15 minutes to improve the performance of the ML model by freely interacting with the chatbot, although they were encouraged to use their hypotheses as a starting point in lieu of asking open-ended questions. They were allowed to ask for code, syntax, definitions, and theories related to the task.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I8.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(7)</span> 
<div id="S3.I8.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I8.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I8.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Participants completed the </span><span id="S3.I8.i7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Final Mental Model Quiz</span><span id="S3.I8.i7.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> to capture changes in their beliefs, while thinking aloud to describe their rationale.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I8.i8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(8)</span> 
<div id="S3.I8.i8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I8.i8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I8.i8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Participants completed the </span><span id="S3.I8.i8.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Subjective Perception Questions</span><span id="S3.I8.i8.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> about their engagement with the chatbot and the task, while thinking aloud once again.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I8.i9" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(9)</span> 
<div id="S3.I8.i9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I8.i9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I8.i9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Repeated steps (4)–(8) for the other task-chatbot pairing, e.g. </span><span id="S3.I8.i9.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">LR</span><span id="S3.I8.i9.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">-</span><span id="S3.I8.i9.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S3.I8.i9.p1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S3.I8.i9.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I8.i10" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(10)</span> 
<div id="S3.I8.i10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I8.i10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I8.i10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Participants discussed their experience in a semi-structured interview, covering their perceptions about the two chatbots and their differences. Throughout the experiment, participants were not explicitly told that the chatbots have been altered to be more or less sycophantic, nor that the chatbots represent different behaviours.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Results</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We first describe our recruited participants and their performance in the task. We then discuss the results for each research question.</span></p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Participants Details</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Out of the 24 participants recruited to the experiment, 7 identified as women, 16 as men, and 1 did not self-disclose. They range between 20-23 years of age (</span><math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=21.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">μ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">21.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=21.0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=0.93" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.93</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=0.93</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) and were all undergraduate students. 21 participants took an introductory ML course at the institution, while three had self-studied with online resources. In terms of baseline AI usage, 15 disclosed that they used AI chatbots daily, while six used them weekly, and three used them infrequently.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">As part of the intake, participants also answered a 5-item, multiple-choice </span><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Knowledge Screening Quiz</span><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> designed to test proficiency on theoretical basics in machine learning. We computed both the accuracy and the Brier score for their performance in the quiz, which factors in both their accuracy and confidence. The lowest Brier score of zero indicates accurate answers and well-calibrated confidence, while higher scores up to 2 indicate poor confidence calibration (being unconfident in correct answers or over-confident in incorrect answers).
The overall range of Brier scores was 0.00-1.23 (</span><math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=0.69" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">μ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.69</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=0.69</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=0.32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=0.32</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) and the number of correctly answered questions ranged from 1-5 (</span><math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=2.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">μ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2.95</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=2.95</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=1.19" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1.19</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=1.19</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">). Only one participant achieved perfect knowledge calibration (100% confidence with all correct answers). See Figure </span><a href="#A4.F2" title="Figure D.2 ‣ Appendix D Knowledge Screening Quiz ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> in the Appendix for the distribution of participants’ Brier scores.
These knowledge screening results demonstrate that most participants had appropriate pre-existing domain knowledge about ML, but were predominantly non-experts, as evidenced by the Brier scores and accuracy. This is crucial as we expect participants to be familiar with ML basics, but demonstrate having some level of misconceptions due to incorrect or missing knowledge.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Performance in ML Debugging Task</h3>

<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03667v1/performance_density_v2.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/318;" width="476" height="318"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>. </span><span id="S4.F3.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Relative improvement in the F1-score performance on a holdout dataset, in comparison to the <span id="S4.F3.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Best</span> (100%) and <span id="S4.F3.10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baseline</span> (0%). </span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We first present the ML debugging performance results to highlight the discrepancy in outcomes between using the </span><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> vs. </span><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> LLM. While task performance is important metric for human-LLM collaboration, it is not a primary research question in our study. We expected a priori that the </span><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot, which exhibits more corrective behaviour, would help participants improve their performance in the task. Instead, we frame our RQs around understanding the mechanisms underlying the development of the performance gap.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We report the relative improvements in the F1-score that participants achieved on their debugged models on a holdout dataset, calculated as </span><math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(F1_{Participant}-F1_{Baseline})/(F1_{Best}-F1_{Baseline})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(F1_{Participant}-F1_{Baseline})/(F1_{Best}-F1_{Baseline})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, where </span><math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F1_{Baseline}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F1_{Baseline}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F1_{Best}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F1_{Best}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> are the scores of the default and fixed models, respectively.</span><span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
            Best possible performance is calculated with a model with all the fixes described in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A ML Debugging Tasks ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> applied. However, better solutions may exist. </span></span></span><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> Participants using the </span><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot achieved a relative F1-score improvement of </span><math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="49.29\pm 41.24\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">49.29</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">±</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">41.24</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">49.29\pm 41.24\%</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. While using the </span><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot, they achieved a lesser improvement of </span><math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.78\pm 62.98\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">4.78</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">±</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">62.98</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.78\pm 62.98\%</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
The density plot showing the distribution is shown in Figure </span><a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.2. Performance in ML Debugging Task ‣ 4. Results ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Users of the </span><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot achieved significantly lower improvement in F1-score, as measured with a paired samples t-test, </span><math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t(23)=-3.38,p=.003" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">23</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">3.38</mn></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">.003</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t(23)=-3.38,p=.003</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.15" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. A one-sample t-test was conducted to determine if the performance gains in </span><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is above 0% (no improvement), finding no significance at </span><math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t(23)=0.36,p=.72" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">23</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.36</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">.72</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t(23)=0.36,p=.72</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In summary, users of the <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span> chatbot did not achieve any improvement in their debugging performance, while <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span> users did.</span><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> In the sections following this, we report the participants’ mental models (</span><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">RQ1</span><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">), workflow behaviours (</span><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">RQ2</span><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">), and subjective perceptions (</span><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">RQ3</span><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">), and detail how the difference in performance can be explained.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T2.12" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>. </span><span id="S4.T2.13" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Regression coefficients, standard errors, and <math id="S4.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-values for the ANCOVA analysis for <span id="S4.T2.13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(A)</span> confidence-weighted accuracy and <span id="S4.T2.13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(B)</span> counts-based accuracy. Bolded quantities indicate a significant <span id="S4.T2.13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">p</span>-value. The presence of sycophancy negatively affects the confidence-weighted accuracy, but does not significantly contribute to the count-based accuracy.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S4.T2.14" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
