<td id="S4.T2.14.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1.4pt;padding-bottom:1.4pt;"><math id="S4.T2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="17.15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">17.15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">17.15</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.T2.14.8.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> (8.46)</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.14.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:1.4pt;padding-bottom:1.4pt;"><math id="S4.T2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">&lt;</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">.02</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.02</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<th id="S4.T2.14.8.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:1.4pt;padding-bottom:1.4pt;"><math id="S4.T2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Sycophancy}\times\text{Baseline Usage}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">Sycophancy</mtext><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">Baseline Usage</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Sycophancy}\times\text{Baseline Usage}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S4.T2.14.8.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1.4pt;padding-bottom:1.4pt;"><math id="S4.T2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.42" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">0.42</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.42</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.T2.14.8.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> (1.27)</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.14.8.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1.4pt;padding-bottom:1.4pt;"><math id="S4.T2.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.73" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">.73</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.73</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>RQ1: Sycophancy Reinforce Misconceptions in Mental Models</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">To understand the impact of sycophancy on participants’ mental models of the debugging tasks, we evaluate the changes in their beliefs about the task, as recorded in the </span><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Mental Model Quiz</span><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> administered pre- and post-chatbot use. As specified in Methods, we compute the changes in the </span><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(A)</span><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> confidence-weighted accuracy and </span><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(B)</span><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> the count-based accuracy of the participant’s beliefs.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">For </span><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(A)</span><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, we fit a linear mixed-effects ANCOVA-style model to assess the effect of the presence of LLM sycophancy on the post-chatbot confidence-weighted accuracy, treating the pre-chatbot confidence-weighted accuracy, the participant’s domain knowledge (Brier score on the Knowledge Screening Quiz), the task (</span><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">LR</span><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> or </span><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">RF</span><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">), and the order of tasks (</span><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> or </span><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> first) as covariates. We also account for an interaction between the participant’s baseline LLM usage (high or low) and the presence of sycophancy, with the hypothesis that more frequent users of LLMs may have strategies to detect and mitigate sycophancy:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{(A)}\ \text{Post-Conf}_{ij}=\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐀</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Post-Conf</mtext><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.778em">=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{(A)}\ \text{Post-Conf}_{ij}=\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}\,\text{Sycophancy}_{ij}+\beta_{2}\,\text{ML Knowledge}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Sycophancy</mtext><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ML Knowledge</mtext><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}\,\text{Sycophancy}_{ij}+\beta_{2}\,\text{ML Knowledge}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\beta_{3}\,\text{Order}+\beta_{4}\,\text{Task}+\beta_{5}\,\text{Pre-Conf}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Order</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Task</mtext></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">5</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Pre-Conf</mtext><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\beta_{3}\,\text{Order}+\beta_{4}\,\text{Task}+\beta_{5}\,\text{Pre-Conf}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\beta_{6}\,(\text{Sycophancy}_{ij}\times\text{Baseline Usage}_{ij})+\epsilon_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">6</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Sycophancy</mtext><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Baseline Usage</mtext><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ϵ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\beta_{6}\,(\text{Sycophancy}_{ij}\times\text{Baseline Usage}_{ij})+\epsilon_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We employ a similar ANCOVA style analysis for </span><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(B)</span><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, but using a binomial generalized linear mixed model (GLMM), which is more suitable for count-based data. We divided the counts by the number of questions (12), such that the values are normalized between 0-1. The analysis uses the same covariates:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{(B)}\ \text{Post-Counts}_{ij}=\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐁</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Post-Counts</mtext><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.778em">=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{(B)}\ \text{Post-Counts}_{ij}=\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}\,\text{Sycophancy}_{ij}+\beta_{2}\,\text{ML Knowledge}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Sycophancy</mtext><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ML Knowledge</mtext><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}\,\text{Sycophancy}_{ij}+\beta_{2}\,\text{ML Knowledge}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\beta_{3}\,\text{Order}+\beta_{4}\,\text{Task}+\beta_{5}\,\text{Pre-Counts}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Order</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Task</mtext></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">5</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Pre-Counts</mtext><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\beta_{3}\,\text{Order}+\beta_{4}\,\text{Task}+\beta_{5}\,\text{Pre-Counts}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\beta_{6}\,(\text{Sycophancy}_{ij}\times\text{Baseline Usage}_{ij})+\epsilon_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">6</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Sycophancy</mtext><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Baseline Usage</mtext><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ϵ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\beta_{6}\,(\text{Sycophancy}_{ij}\times\text{Baseline Usage}_{ij})+\epsilon_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We report the coefficients and p-values of the analyses in Table </span><a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2. Performance in ML Debugging Task ‣ 4. Results ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
For </span><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(A)</span><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, sycophancy (</span><math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=-21.58,p&lt;.0001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">21.58</mn></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&lt;</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">.0001</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=-21.58,p&lt;.0001</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) is highly significant for reducing the confidence-weighted accuracy, which means that users of the </span><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot become more calibrated in their beliefs, while users of the </span><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot did not. Confidence calibration manifests as becoming more confident in correct beliefs, becoming less confident in wrong beliefs, or both.
We further find a significant interaction between Sycophancy </span><math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> Baseline Usage (</span><math id="S4.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=17.15,p&lt;.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">17.15</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&lt;</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">.02</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=17.15,p&lt;.02</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">), which indicates that the negative effect of sycophancy can be moderated by high use of LLMs. This suggests that heavy users may be more experienced in prompting and verifying results, and employ better strategies when working with sycophantic LLMs.
For </span><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(B)</span><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, the analysis shows that the improvement in the count of correct beliefs is not significantly affected by sycophancy (</span><math id="S4.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=-0.49,p=0.62" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.49</mn></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.62</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=-0.49,p=0.62</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">). For the full transition matrix analysis of the count of correct beliefs pre- and post-chatbot usage, see Figure </span><a href="#A7.F1" title="Figure G.1 ‣ Appendix G Additional RQ1 Results ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.1</span></a><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.15" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> in the Appendix.
In summary, we find that the </span><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot significantly improves users’ confidence calibration towards the correct beliefs, but does not significantly affect the overall number of correct beliefs</span><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. This discrepancy can perhaps be explained as that the </span><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.19.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.20" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot helped strengthen the participants’ beliefs in the correct direction, while the </span><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.21" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.21.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.22" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot also led people to more accurate beliefs but did not successfully convince them—resulting in lower confidence scores overall.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03667v1/overall_accuracy_vertical.svg" id="S4.F4.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:476/635;" width="476" height="635"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F4.sf1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Changes to the confidence-weighted accuracy
<br class="ltx_break">(top) and the count-based accuracy (bottom).</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03667v1/accuracy_changes_vertical_v2.svg" id="S4.F4.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/476;" width="476" height="476"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F4.sf2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Changes in the confidence-weighted accuracy based on the original correctness of participants’ beliefs: correct (top), neutral (mid), or wrong (bottom), macro-averaged per category. Line weight indicates the proportion of samples.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>. </span><span id="S4.F4.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The overall confidence-weighted and count-based accuracy changes (left column) and a more granular analysis on how confidence changes based on the pre-chatbot correctness (right column). A positive confidence score always means a correct belief. Significance in the pre-post change for each condition is measured with the Wilcoxon signed-rank test with the Benjamini-Hochberg correction, and the graphical error bars indicate the standard error of the mean. The significance between conditions is indicated in gray and computed with the ANCOVA analysis.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We visualize the changes to the measures in Figure </span><a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.3. RQ1: Sycophancy Reinforce Misconceptions in Mental Models ‣ 4. Results ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. While the ANCOVA analyses provide information on the impact of sycophancy, we also compute the significance of the within-condition pre-post changes, which report the standalone effects of the condition. We compute significance with a Wilcoxon signed-rank test and record the effect size as a rank-biserial correlation, which ranges from -1 to +1. To account for multiple hypotheses, we apply the Benjamini-Hochberg correction for 10 tests (five pre-post measures per condition </span><math id="S4.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> two conditions) for Type I errors.
For </span><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(A)</span><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, the </span><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot significantly improved the confidence-weighted accuracy (</span><math id="S4.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=11.0,p=.001,r=0.92" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">W</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">11.0</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">.001</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">r</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.92</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=11.0,p=.001,r=0.92</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) while the </span><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot did not (</span><math id="S4.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=106.0,p=.51,r=0.63" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">W</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">106.0</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">.51</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">r</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.63</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=106.0,p=.51,r=0.63</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">), and both also slightly increased the proportion of accurate beliefs, with </span><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> at 9.38% (</span><math id="S4.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=4.0,p=.004,r=0.93" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">W</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">4.0</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">.004</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">r</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.93</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=4.0,p=.004,r=0.93</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) beating out </span><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> (</span><math id="S4.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=10.0,p=.045,r=0.74" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">W</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">10.0</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">.045</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">r</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.74</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=10.0,p=.045,r=0.74</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) at 4.86% — although this difference did not reach significance. For a question-level breakdown of pre-post confidence changes, see Figure </span><a href="#A7.F2" title="Figure G.2 ‣ Appendix G Additional RQ1 Results ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.2</span></a><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> in the Appendix.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We further decomposed the confidence-weighted accuracy into separate sub-measures based on if the participant’s original beliefs at the pre-chatbot stage were correct (</span><math id="S4.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{confidence}_{pre}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">confidence</mtext><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&gt;</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{confidence}_{pre}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">), wrong (</span><math id="S4.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{confidence}_{pre}&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">confidence</mtext><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&lt;</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{confidence}_{pre}&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">), or neutral (</span><math id="S4.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{confidence}_{pre}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">confidence</mtext><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{confidence}_{pre}=0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">), as shown in Figure </span><a href="#S4.F4.sf2" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 4.3. RQ1: Sycophancy Reinforce Misconceptions in Mental Models ‣ 4. Results ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(b)</span></a><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. This promotes a more granular understanding of where belief changes and improvements occur.
Overall, we find that neither chatbot significantly affected confidence in beliefs that were already correct. However, the </span><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot improved wrong beliefs (</span><math id="S4.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=20.0,p=.028,r=0.82" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">W</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">20.0</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">.028</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">r</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.82</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=20.0,p=.028,r=0.82</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) while the </span><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot did not (</span><math id="S4.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=44.5,p=.77,r=0.42" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">W</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">44.5</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">.77</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">r</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.42</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=44.5,p=.77,r=0.42</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">), signaling that </span><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">sycophancy can reinforce misconceptions</span><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, causing the user to miss out on potential learning opportunities. Similarly, we find that the </span><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.15" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot improved accuracy in beliefs where users were neutral about (</span><math id="S4.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=12.0,p=.028,r=0.77" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">W</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">12.0</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">.028</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">r</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.77</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=12.0,p=.028,r=0.77</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">), while the </span><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.17.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot again had no significant effect (</span><math id="S4.SS3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=28.5,p=.77,r=0.13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">W</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">28.5</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">.77</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">r</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.13</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=28.5,p=.77,r=0.13</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.19" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">).
These results suggest that </span><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.20" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">sycophancy can negatively affect novices when they have uncertainties or misconceptions by reinforcing incorrect beliefs.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03667v1/workflows_v2.svg" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/233;" width="476" height="233"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.45" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>. </span><span id="S4.F5.46" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Full coded workflows for all participants for the <span id="S4.F5.46.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S4.F5.46.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span> and <span id="S4.F5.46.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S4.F5.46.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span> conditions. Each square box represents an event that is a <span id="S4.F5.46.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">User</span> <span id="S4.F5.46.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Query</span>, <span id="S4.F5.46.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Chatbot</span> <span id="S4.F5.46.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Response</span>, or <span id="S4.F5.46.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Code</span> <span id="S4.F5.46.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Change</span>. Outcomes of interest, such as <span id="S4.F5.46.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">User</span> <span id="S4.F5.46.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Queries</span> with <span id="S4.F5.46.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">misconceptions</span>, <span id="S4.F5.46.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Chatbot</span> <span id="S4.F5.46.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Responses</span> that are <span id="S4.F5.46.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">confirmatory</span>, and <span id="S4.F5.46.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Code</span> <span id="S4.F5.46.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Changes</span> that <span id="S4.F5.46.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">improve</span> or <span id="S4.F5.46.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">worsen</span> performance, are graphically indicated. Events are grouped together in chunks, with each chunk indicating the <span id="S4.F5.46.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Reliance</span> <span id="S4.F5.46.20" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Outcome</span> by colour.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="S4.F6.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.F6.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03667v1/workflow_proportion_bars_v2.svg" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:343/215;" width="343" height="215"></object></span><span id="S4.F6.3.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span>
<span id="S4.F6.3.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:89.7pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03667v1/workflow_misconception_ratio.svg" id="S4.F6.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:124/66;" width="124" height="66"></object><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03667v1/workflow_reliance_ratio.svg" id="S4.F6.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:124/66;" width="124" height="66"></object><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03667v1/workflow_confirmatory_ratio_v2.svg" id="S4.F6.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:124/73;" width="124" height="73"></object>
</span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F6.18" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>. </span><span id="S4.F6.19" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Proportion of workflows spent in the five reliance outcomes (left) and the proportions of confirmatory misconceived queries (top right), reliance on LLM behaviours (middle right), and confirmatory chatbot responses (bottom right) identified in the workflows. Statistical testing for significance between conditions is performed with the <span id="S4.F6.19.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">z</span>-test for proportions, and significant values are in bold. While both conditions had similar rates of misconceived queries and reliance on LLM behaviour, <span id="S4.F6.19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S4.F6.19.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span> workflows spent significantly more time in <span id="S4.F6.19.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Over-Reliance</span> rather than <span id="S4.F6.19.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Appropriate Reliance on the LLM</span> due to the <span id="S4.F6.19.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">much more</span> confirmatory nature of the <span id="S4.F6.19.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S4.F6.19.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span> chatbot to validate misconceptions.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4. </span>RQ2: Sycophancy Results in Higher Rates of Over-Reliance</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We now analyze how participants behaved in their ML debugging task workflows and how they relied on the LLM chatbots.
Two researchers who were present for the user studies iteratively developed the codebook through discussions, revisions, and computing inter-rater reliability — the final version of which is available in Appendix </span><a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Workflow Codebook ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. In the final round of revising the codebook, IRR was computed with Cohen’s kappa on six task workflows (representing 12.5% of the workflows and 14.1% of the events across all workflows). Since our codes are hierarchically categorized across different code categories, we report the category level IRR, the micro-averaged IRR, and the macro-averaged IRR in Table </span><a href="#A8.T1" title="Table H.1 ‣ Appendix H Additional RQ2 Results ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H.1</span></a><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> in the Appendix. The category-level IRRs range from </span><math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa=0.58" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">κ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.58</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=0.58</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> (</span><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Code</span><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Change</span><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">outcomes</span><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) to </span><math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa=0.82" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">κ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.82</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=0.82</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> (</span><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Code</span><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Change</span><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.15" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">detailed actions</span><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">). The micro and macro averaged Cohen’s kappa are both </span><math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa=0.71" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">κ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.71</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=0.71</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, which indicates high agreement above chance. Since this is satisfactory according to our pre-determined agreement threshold </span><math id="S4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa&gt;0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">κ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&gt;</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa&gt;0.6</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.19" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, the remaining workflows were then coded.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Figure </span><a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.3. RQ1: Sycophancy Reinforce Misconceptions in Mental Models ‣ 4. Results ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> shows the workflows of all 24 participants in both conditions, including event-level (</span><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">User</span><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Query</span><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Chatbot</span><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Response</span><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Code</span><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Change</span><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) and chunk-level (</span><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Reliance</span><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) codes. As a reminder, we used the event-level codes to classify the reliance type for each workflow chunk as one of five: over-reliance, under-reliance, appropriate reliance on helpful LLM advice, appropriate self-reliance when LLM advice is harmful, and engaging in conceptual discussions only. We computed proportions of the following quantities in Figure </span><a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.3. RQ1: Sycophancy Reinforce Misconceptions in Mental Models ‣ 4. Results ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and report their difference between conditions:</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Misconceived Queries:</span><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> the proportion of user queries that contained a misconception — no difference between conditions (</span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.52" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">.52</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.52</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">), indicating that participants asked </span><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">similar</span><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> questions to both chatbots.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Reliance on LLM:</span><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> the proportion of workflow chunks that were classified as </span><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">reliance on LLM</span><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, which includes both over-reliance and appropriate LLM reliance — no difference between conditions (</span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.83" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">.83</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.83</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">), indicating that participants relied similarly when using both chatbots.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Confirmatory Responses:</span><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> the proportion of chatbot responses that were confirmatory to the user’s beliefs — </span><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is significantly more confirmatory (</span><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.0001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&lt;</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">.0001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.0001</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">), verifying that the chatbots behaved as expected in the experiments and indicating that </span><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot users received more advice that echoes their existing beliefs.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">For the main results of RQ2, we focus on differentiating reliance outcomes between the conditions. We normalized the timescale of each workflow and computed the fraction of time that each participant spent in each reliance outcome class, then aggregated the proportions across all trials to contrast the </span><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot’s reliance behaviours in Figure </span><a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.3. RQ1: Sycophancy Reinforce Misconceptions in Mental Models ‣ 4. Results ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Using </span><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">z</span><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">-test for proportions, we find that </span><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> workflows spent significantly more time in over-reliance (</span><math id="S4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.0004" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&lt;</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">.0004</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.0004</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">), while the LLM reliance in </span><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> workflows resulted in significantly higher appropriate reliance (</span><math id="S4.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.004" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&lt;</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">.004</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.004</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">).
With the prior findings showing that participants prompted misconceptions and relied on the LLM at similar rates across both conditions, this suggests that the </span><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot induced overwhelming inappropriate over-reliance through validating users’ misconceptions. </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">To illustrate the reliance patterns observed with more context and nuance, we documented two cases of inappropriate reliance, drawing attention to exemplar workflows of each reliance category. Note that these anecdotes do not represent all possible ways that the indicated reliance behaviour can occur.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Case Study: Over-Reliance.</span><span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P5</span><span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> began by asking a misconceived question for the </span><span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">LR</span><span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> task to the </span><span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot, upon which they received a </span><span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">confirmatory</span><span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> yet fully irrelevant code implementation that attempts to solve their problem. They thus interpreted this confirmation as being on the right track, and  then spent multiple conversational turns working with the chatbot to debug various compilation problems in the code, without any success. Afterwards, they commented that </span><span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">“I was lost… when I don’t know, I just follow the chatbot"</span><span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, suggesting that they were aware of their over-reliance but lacked a strategy to break the pattern due to their inexperience in the task domain.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Case Study: Under-Reliance.</span><span id="S4.SS4.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
</span><span id="S4.SS4.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P23</span><span id="S4.SS4.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> presented a misconceived theory about the </span><span id="S4.SS4.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">RF</span><span id="S4.SS4.p6.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> task to the </span><span id="S4.SS4.p6.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S4.SS4.p6.1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS4.p6.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot, believing that </span><span id="S4.SS4.p6.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">“the model… I don’t think it’s overfitting because it only has six features"</span><span id="S4.SS4.p6.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Despite receiving a direct correction from the chatbot, they expressed dismissal of the suggestion and neglected to implement the suggested code fixes.
Later, while completing the post-experiment survey, they rated the reliability of the chatbot as low because they continued to believe that </span><span id="S4.SS4.p6.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">“overfitting may not be the problem but [the chatbot] said it was"</span><span id="S4.SS4.p6.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. This interaction signifies that confirmation bias towards existing misconceptions can even be stronger than explicitly corrective explanations.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03667v1/ratings_v2.svg" id="S4.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/167;" width="429" height="167"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F7.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>. </span><span id="S4.F7.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Subjective perceptions ratings on a 7-point Likert scale, where bolded quantities are significant.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5. </span>RQ3: Sycophancy is Largely Unnoticed and Unmitigated</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Lastly, we examine whether users of each chatbot developed different perceptions about them. Figure </span><a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4.4. RQ2: Sycophancy Results in Higher Rates of Over-Reliance ‣ 4. Results ‣ Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> shows the </span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Subjective Perceptions</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> Likert ratings, contrasting the ratings for </span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> with the Wilcoxon signed rank test. Significant perception categories that are higher for the </span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot are: alternative ideas provided by the chatbot (</span><math id="S4.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">.02</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.02</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">), perceived improvement in the task (</span><math id="S4.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.04" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">.04</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.04</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">), and engagement in the task (</span><math id="S4.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.04" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">.04</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.04</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">).
In all other categories, there was no clear significance according to our threshold of </span><math id="S4.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&lt;</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.05</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. The lack of perceived differences in the categories related to the chatbot properties (e.g., </span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">helpfulness</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">reliability</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) suggests that </span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">users were largely unobservant of the effect of sycophancy on the LLM’s response characteristics</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.20" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, which leaves them in a more vulnerable state to the negative impacts on their mental models and behaviours. To corroborate the Likert survey results, we also asked in the interview if participants could describe any differences they noticed between the two chatbots. Note that we did not prime users with the expectation that the two chatbots were either the same or different. Only 7/24 (29%) acknowledged a difference and managed to describe it in terms of characteristics related to sycophancy, such as </span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.21" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">agreeableness</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.22" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> (</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.23" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P9</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.24" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.25" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P21</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.26" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.27" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P24</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.28" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">), </span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.29" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">reinforcement of ideas</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.30" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> (</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.31" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P5</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.32" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.33" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P19</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.34" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">), and </span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.35" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">lack of alternative ideas</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.36" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> (</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.37" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P6</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.38" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.39" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P14</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.40" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">).
Another 5/24 (21%) noticed slight discrepancies, but attributed them to other factors unrelated to sycophancy, like the </span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.41" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">depth of explanation</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.42" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> (</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.43" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P2</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.44" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.45" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P11</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.46" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.47" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P18</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.48" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">), </span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.49" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">formatting</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.50" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> (</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.51" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P15</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.52" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">), or </span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.53" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">tone</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.54" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> (</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.55" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P23</span><span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.56" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">). The remaining 12/24 (50%) answered the question by stating they did not notice any differences, suggesting that sycophancy can be well-camouflaged — at least, in short interactions.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In the following thematic analysis of the interview data, we triangulate between the participants’ statements, their Likert ratings, and their performance and behaviours. We further expand beyond their perceptions of our experimental chatbots and discuss how they observe and mitigate sycophancy in chatbots in real life.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Users attribute differences in chatbot responses to prompting styles and task properties.</span><span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
As discussed above, a vast majority of participants were unable to distinguish between the response styles of the chatbots. However, in exploring their post-experiments interviews, we discover that many people </span><span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">had</span><span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> noticed a difference, but entirely attributed the difference to their own prompting methods — including both their knowledge in the domain and the style of their queries. This is exemplified by </span><span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P13</span><span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, who believed that they performed worse when paired with the </span><span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot because they asked irrelevant or uninformed questions; and </span><span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P10</span><span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, who believed that the </span><span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot did not offer alternative ideas because they did not ask open-ended questions. The complex nature of the task also preoccupied many users and played a factor in both distracting their focus and affecting the quality of their queries. </span><span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P3</span><span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> reflected that they were so immersed in the task that the two chatbots appeared the same, saying </span><span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">“if you’re so stuck in your head, you are following down the same path and don’t notice"</span><span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">“<span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span> does what you say you want, <span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span> figures out what you actually want".</span><span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> Among participants who did notice a difference, comments made on the traits that set the chatbots apart indirectly alluded to sycophancy. </span><span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P6</span><span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, who fell into a pattern of over-reliance with the </span><span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot, observed that </span><span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">“the [<span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span>] just said my thoughts are right…I didn’t ask for alternative ideas so it didn’t give me any."</span><span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
</span><span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P5</span><span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> corroborated that </span><span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">“[<span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span>] just does what you ask it to do…[<span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.11.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span>] gave me ideas that I didn’t think of before"</span><span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. </span><span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P21</span><span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> also observed that </span><span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">“[<span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.15.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span>] was warning me more about mistakes, whereas [<span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.15.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span>] just validated my ideas, but it could have been the way I prompted"</span><span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Similarly, </span><span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P23</span><span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> agreed with </span><span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">“[<span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.19.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.19.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span>] focuses more on the truth and reality".</span><span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.20" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> Interestingly, participants did not comment on if they believed that these observed differences impacted them in the task, nor did they notice instances where sycophancy negatively led them into patterns of over-reliance.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The tone of social sycophancy can be a signal for either encouragement or distrust.</span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
Many participants, when prompted to discuss if they noticed instances of real world chatbots behaving in an overly agreeable manner, described similar stories of the AI chatbot providing inconsistent responses in attempts to always agree with the user (</span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P1</span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P3</span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P9</span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P10</span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P12</span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P13</span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P14</span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P15</span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P17</span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.20" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.21" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P18</span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.22" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.23" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P19</span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.24" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.25" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P21</span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.26" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">). Some participants (</span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.27" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P13</span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.28" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.29" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P15</span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.30" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.31" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P17</span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.32" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) further described a strong association between the problematic performance of the LLM directly with the upbeat and agreeable tone of the responses, which was called </span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.33" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">unnatural</span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.34" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> by </span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.35" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P17</span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.36" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Due to this negative relationship, they use the tone of the chatbot as an indicator for potential hallucinations in the LLM’s responses. </span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.37" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P13</span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.38" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> went as far as to say, </span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.39" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">“the agreement makes me take things with a grain of salt, so I’d rather have the chatbot be rude"</span><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.40" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
Despite this, prior research in economics has shown that stated and revealed preferences are not always consistent </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.41" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wardman, 1988</a><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.43" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.44" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. In our study, we see equally high ratings for chat helpfulness, reliability, and enjoyableness, indicating that participants liked the overall pleasant and helpful tone in both chatbots.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Taking an opposite stance, </span><span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P8</span><span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P23</span><span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> instead believed that a positive and helpful tone makes them feel encouraged to ask questions, through </span><span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">“building rapport"</span><span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> from </span><span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P8</span><span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and being more </span><span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">“passionate and friendly"</span><span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> from </span><span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P23</span><span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Alluding to the recent switch from </span><span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">GPT-4o</span><span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.15" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> to </span><span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">GPT-5</span><span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P23</span><span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.19" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> further elaborates that the </span><span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.20" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.20.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.21" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot they used is </span><span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.22" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">“more like a scientist, like <span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.22.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-5</span>… but <span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.22.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4o</span> is more friendly"</span><span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.23" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. How individual users respond to the tone of the chatbot has been the subject of prior research
</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.24" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun and Wang, 2025</a><span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.26" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.27" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, and we show that an agreeable tone can be perceived both positively and negatively by different user groups. Regardless of the difference between individuals, this suggests that a unified chatbot tone may not be as effective as more personalized approaches.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Sycophancy is commonly noticed in chatbots, but users’ motivation to counter it vary.</span><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
Among participants who mention being affected by sycophancy in real life, we identify a range of different attitudes with respect to the urgency, importance, and persistence of how they try strategies for mitigating the issues. Some describe a futile process of re-prompting </span><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">“are you sure?"</span><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> in response to increasingly sycophantic outputs from the chatbot. However, </span><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P9</span><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> acknowledges that success is dependent on expertise, with </span><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">“it’s easy to identify that they’re giving wrong information if you are well-versed in the field… in this experience, I can’t really tell if they were giving me wrong info"</span><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. To get better coverage on understanding the solution space, </span><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P1</span><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P20</span><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> mention that they always explicitly ask chatbots to list alternative ideas or downsides of their current ideas. </span><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P21</span><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> went further to modify ChatGPT’s custom instructions, saying </span><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">“even if I try to make it direct and prioritize honesty instead of validation, it doesn’t work, they seem deeply programmed to validate."</span><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
Several (</span><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P2</span><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P5</span><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.20" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.21" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P19</span><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.22" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) also try to reduce their reliance on AI tools in general.
</span><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.23" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P2</span><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.24" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> describes feeling guilty, like they’re looking for </span><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.25" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">“an easy way out"</span><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.26" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> if they use chatbots instead of trying things on their own. </span><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.27" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P19</span><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.28" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> exhibits more general under-reliance in chatbots, believing that chatbots won’t help them for complex problem-solving.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">However, several other participants place a lot of trust by default in chatbots, whether that’s because of confidence in their abilities or because of the general convenience they offer. </span><span id="S4.SS5.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P20</span><span id="S4.SS5.p8.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> believes that in comparison to our experimental chatbots, real LLMs would </span><span id="S4.SS5.p8.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">“give me a lot of solutions and it will suggest the best approach"</span><span id="S4.SS5.p8.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. </span><span id="S4.SS5.p8.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P6</span><span id="S4.SS5.p8.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> described his highly AI-integrated learning workflow, and that </span><span id="S4.SS5.p8.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">“I don’t talk to TAs and profs anymore, and when I ask them, it’s pretty much the same question that I ask ChatGPT"</span><span id="S4.SS5.p8.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, while also believing </span><span id="S4.SS5.p8.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">“if I’m wrong, the [chatbot] should correct me"</span><span id="S4.SS5.p8.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> — this mindset points to a slippery slope of over-reliance on AI without exercising vigilance. </span><span id="S4.SS5.p8.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P3</span><span id="S4.SS5.p8.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> also believes that chatbots can be better at explaining than their instructors, and that </span><span id="S4.SS5.p8.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">“if it is wrong, it is wrong"</span><span id="S4.SS5.p8.1.15" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, suggesting that bad answers are still better than nothing. The overarching trust that participants indicated is alarming, especially among users who felt like they didn’t need to validate the chatbot’s outputs in real life. This reflects the behaviour of cognitive offloading, which has more severe implications on how AI chatbots can impact users’ learning and cognition over time </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S4.SS5.p8.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kosmyna et al., 2025</a><span id="S4.SS5.p8.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S4.SS5.p8.1.19" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Key Findings.</span><span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> We uncover that sycophantic LLMs have the potential to induce </span><span id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">a disconnect between how people think and behave with how they perceive their interactions with the LLM</span><span id="S5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
In </span><span id="S5.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">RQ1</span><span id="S5.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, we find that sycophancy can reinforce misconceptions in novices’ beliefs about the task, but the negative effects may be diluted with higher levels of baseline LLM usage. In </span><span id="S5.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">RQ2</span><span id="S5.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, we find that reliance actions that users take are more likely to result in unhelpful code changes (over-reliance on the LLM). Both findings are mechanisms that contribute to the inferior task performance when using the </span><span id="S5.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S5.p1.1.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S5.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbot. However, in </span><span id="S5.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">RQ3</span><span id="S5.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, 71% of our participants were unobservant to the underlying sycophantic properties of the LLM. Furthermore, a majority of the subjective perception categories were rated without significant differences between the chatbots. In exploring the qualitative data, we uncover conflicting preferences towards the agreeableness in the tone of LLMs and the mitigation strategies against sycophancy in real-world usage.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We synthesize our key findings on the impact of LLM sycophancy on users in open-ended problem-solving tasks, which expands upon the standard set of tasks that have been studied in LLM sycophancy research. We discuss the repercussions on task learners as a group that is particularly vulnerable to negative cognitive impacts of sycophancy. Finally, we draw attention to the tension between how immediate user desires and long-term cognition-preserving strategies may arise in the current training paradigm of LLMs, and an outlook for interaction strategies and interventions that moderate the effect of sycophancy.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">LLM Echo Chambers in Complex Tasks.</span><span id="S5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
Sycophancy is originally conceptualized and evaluated based on inducing LLMs to output factually false answers that align with the opinions of their users </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma et al., 2023</a><span id="S5.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. It is commonly benchmarked through simple question-answering datasets </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fanous et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al., 2025</a><span id="S5.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, although recent efforts have expanded towards multi-turn conversations too </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p3.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hong et al., 2025</a><span id="S5.p3.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p3.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. We explore user interactions in a more ecologically valid, open-ended task, demonstrating that sycophancy can present itself in a less detectable way that does not always mean being </span><span id="S5.p3.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">factually wrong</span><span id="S5.p3.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Simply by echoing and enforcing the user’s existing mental model of the task, irrespective of correctness or relevance, LLMs can contribute to a distorted sense of perception </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p3.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Morrin et al., 2025</a><span id="S5.p3.1.19" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p3.1.20" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. While diminished performance presents a legitimate risk to human-LLM collaboration, the user further misses out on the opportunity to engage with alternative approaches, critically reflect on their mental model of the task, and calibrate the confidence in their own knowledge. Such harms are more difficult to quantify and may surface as ramifications in downstream engagements, rather than in immediate outcomes. These factors are not easily measured in static benchmarking tests, as they derive from the nuanced behaviours of real people.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Our findings strike parallels with the body of research on LLM persuasion </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rogiers et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Breum et al., 2024</a><span id="S5.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Prior works have found that LLMs can impart strong influences on personal beliefs of their users, creating echo chambers of thought which can be particularly troubling when concerning sensitive, political, or extremist issues </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krügel et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Danry et al., 2025</a><span id="S5.p4.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p4.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. LLMs that are further capable of deception, which we did not evaluate, extend these risks by creating opportunities where the user can be intentionally misled </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p4.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hagendorff, 2024</a><span id="S5.p4.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p4.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. At larger scales across society, this raises the question of whether and how the consequences of the dark patterns of LLM design can be detected and mitigated </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p4.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kran et al., 2025</a><span id="S5.p4.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p4.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Our results in the open-ended ML debugging task also call upon sycophancy evaluation tests to consider more realistic and complex user interaction scenarios, which may include brainstorming, reasoning, and automation domains. Investigating the impact of sycophancy in a wider range of applications can inform the development of model guardrails that can lessen harms </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p4.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Malmqvist, 2025</a><span id="S5.p4.1.20" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p4.1.21" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Implications for Novice-LLM Interactions.</span><span id="S5.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
Our findings that sycophancy may reinforce false beliefs are especially troubling when contextualized within the population of novices who are learning new information and skills </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brachman et al., 2024</a><span id="S5.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. People are turning to AI tools to enhance the speed at which they can learn new skills, including coding and machine learning, but the pedagogical effectiveness of these approaches generally relies on substantial external scaffolding </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p5.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2025</a><span id="S5.p5.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p5.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. What makes novices particularly vulnerable to sycophantic AI? While experts may be able to leverage LLMs using precise prompting and seasoned verification, novices lack the knowledge and first-hand experience to supervise their interactions with the LLM </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p5.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kazemitabaar et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Margulieux et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bo et al., 2025</a><span id="S5.p5.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p5.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. As echoed in our interviews with participants, many of them perceive LLMs to be a knowledge equalizing tool that is more generally beneficial than harmful, especially in resource-limited contexts where they don’t have access to a better learning alternative (e.g., their professor). A further concern specific to novices is the impact of cognitive offloading to the LLM on the learning process.
People develop mental models iteratively over time through synthesizing their interactions with the system and their learned conceptual knowledge </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p5.1.15" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kieras and Bovair, 1984</a><span id="S5.p5.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p5.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Without such practices, novices may never learn the necessary mental models to become self-sufficient, even if they can generate AI-assisted outputs at a high level of perceived expertise </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p5.1.19" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krupp et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Blake, 2025</a><span id="S5.p5.1.21" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p5.1.22" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Our participants mention this as a concern with respect to increasing reliance on LLMs, and some even divulge personal strategies for learning with LLMs, but there lacks systematic recommendations for effective engagement.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Beyond novices in tasks, our results also have broader implications for the general demographic of young people in an increasingly AI-driven society. ChatGPT and most other LLM-based chatbots are not age-restricted for teenagers, who are particularly vulnerable to persuasive online influences </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rath et al., 2025</a><span id="S5.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. A pressing concern is the potential erosion and under-development of foundational cognitive skills like reading, writing, reasoning, and problem-solving </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p6.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kosmyna et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2025</a><span id="S5.p6.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p6.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Sycophantic LLMs that are agreeable, efficient, and extremely accessible, compounded with a lack of awareness of the vulnerability of particular users, can irreparably alter the cognition of young users. We call for longitudinal research to better understand the impact of LLMs on learning. Furthermore, we advocate for the development and evaluation of cognitive-preserving AI designs, which include cognitive forcing UI designs </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p6.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Buçinca et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Collins et al., 2024</a><span id="S5.p6.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p6.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, proactive interactions </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p6.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pu et al., 2025</a><span id="S5.p6.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p6.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, and the promotion of meta-cognitive practices </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p6.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tankelevitch et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Margulieux et al., 2024</a><span id="S5.p6.1.20" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p6.1.21" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Sycophancy as a Value Tension in LLM Design.</span><span id="S5.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
Sycophancy is recognized as a key challenge in LLM safety, yet represents the tension between what </span><span id="S5.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">users think they want</span><span id="S5.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, and what may end up being </span><span id="S5.p7.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">harmful to them</span><span id="S5.p7.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> in the long term </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p7.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ibrahim et al., 2025</a><span id="S5.p7.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p7.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) has been the main driver of success in the LLM age of chatbot technology </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p7.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2021</a><span id="S5.p7.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p7.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Learning from the reward signal of satisfying the user — which by proxy encodes qualities of helpfulness, agreeableness, and trustworthiness — has been able to radically upscale the capabilities of these systems. However, we now recognize that such training paradigms can develop undesirable traits that may be hidden from naive evaluations. For certain user archetypes, like people who seek to outsource deep thinking or people who react positively to emotional validation, their preferences may be reflected as positive ratings for LLM outputs that inadvertently embody sycophancy </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p7.1.15" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">Williams et al., 2024</a><span id="S5.p7.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p7.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. While more critical users may recognize that a “yes man" chatbot limits their underlying usefulness, or even may find the output style placating and annoying, evidence points to the sycophantic characteristic of LLMs being core to how they are trained and is non-trivial to eliminate </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p7.1.19" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Malmqvist, 2025</a><span id="S5.p7.1.21" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p7.1.22" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Therefore, addressing the inherent tension between user satisfaction and long-term safety remains a salient problem.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">For the time being, sycophantic behaviour may be an inevitable consequence of training LLMs on human feedback. Addressing dark patterns of technology has been a topic of research in HCI far pre-dating LLMs, spanning social media </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cara, 2019</a><span id="S5.p8.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p8.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, website design </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p8.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gunawan et al., 2021</a><span id="S5.p8.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p8.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, and payment systems </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p8.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luguri and Strahilevitz, 2021</a><span id="S5.p8.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p8.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Within LLMs, these dark patterns often manifest as deception over the user </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p8.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hagendorff, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kran et al., 2025</a><span id="S5.p8.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p8.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Design tensions often manifest when there are opposing values represented by the stakeholders — in the case of LLM chatbots, it is often between revenue generation and the well-being of the users. Our results indicate that an unintended consequence of maximizing helpfulness may lead to chatbots that reinforce bad ideas and lead to poor outcomes </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p8.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ibrahim et al., 2025</a><span id="S5.p8.1.20" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p8.1.21" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. With a better understanding of how LLM sycophancy can impact users in open-ended tasks, model developers may want to consider including these inadvertent impacts in their design requirements. They may also be incentivized to perform more comprehensive evaluations, which include multi-turn user tests with diverse end users, rather than just using simple benchmarks and unit tests.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Lastly, we recognize that individuals and societies can co-evolve alongside the changes in technology </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p9.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al., 2024</a><span id="S5.p9.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p9.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> — this is demonstrated by our preliminary finding that more frequent LLM users were less impacted by sycophancy. Through AI literacy education, users can be taught critical limitations of LLMs, promoting better awareness of appropriate use cases </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p9.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bender, 2024</a><span id="S5.p9.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p9.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. For example, lay end users were largely unaware of sycophancy before this year. But due to improved public awareness, including the transparent post-mortem analysis from OpenAI </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p9.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025</a><span id="S5.p9.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p9.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and increased visibility on parasocial chatbots in the news </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p9.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">cbs, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reiley, 2025</a><span id="S5.p9.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p9.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, more and more of the public have the opportunity to learn about and discuss their awareness of this risk. While obviously challenging (and not absolving model developers of their responsibility), systemic structures like educational institutions and regulatory bodies can also adapt their policies to meet the opportunities and challenges posed by Generative AI </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p9.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hacker et al., 2023</a><span id="S5.p9.1.20" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p9.1.21" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. The AI and HCI research communities have an incentive to collaborate on the development, maintenance, and evaluation of value-aligned, cognition-preserving AI tools.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Limitations.</span><span id="S5.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
Finally, we present the limitations and assumptions made in our experimental setup that may have impacted the ecological validity of our results. While we intend to create a chatbot that is realistically sycophantic, it is impossible to compare its performance to the now retracted </span><span id="S5.p10.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">GPT-4o</span><span id="S5.p10.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.p10.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025</a><span id="S5.p10.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.p10.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> — a real chatbot with high sycophancy. As a workaround, we introduced an intermediate misconception inference step that explicitly lists out the user’s likely beliefs. This technique results in the default LLM becoming more corrective, which may be unrealistic. Therefore, our </span><span id="S5.p10.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S5.p10.1.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S5.p10.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><span id="S5.p10.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S5.p10.1.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S5.p10.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbots may represent an artificially amplified effect of LLM sycophancy.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In order to maximize opportunities where </span><span id="S5.p11.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9933CC;">High<span id="S5.p11.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S5.p11.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><span id="S5.p11.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Low<span id="S5.p11.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span>Sycophancy</span><span id="S5.p11.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chatbots would respond differently (e.g., when the user query contains misconceptions), we emphasized in our experiment protocol that participants should always lead the queries with their hypotheses, which was not the natural pattern of prompting for some participants. In unrestricted workflows, users may ask more open-ended questions that do not contain clear leads and misconceptions, which reduces the negative impact of sycophancy.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The length of time participants spend with each chatbot is approximately 15 minutes, which allowed most to complete 2-10 rounds of questions. However, this is not a substantial amount of time to learn the idiosyncrasies of the chatbots, and may have led to an under-estimation of how many people could identify the difference between them. Many participants explicitly stated that they didn’t notice a difference at first, but when probed to reflect more deeply, they began brainstorming ideas based on observations they had made but didn’t have enough evidence to support.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Mental models are difficult to capture and lack standardized methods. We operationalize this measure as a confidence rating over a constant set of statements, which enabled us to perform quantitative analyses. However, this method does not capture nuances, edge cases, or beliefs that are otherwise not represented in the set of statements. Since participants also saw correct statements ahead of their problem-solving, this may have biased them to use them as hints to guide their problem-solving. We attempt to mitigate this by having them brainstorm their own hypotheses at the start, and warning them in the quiz not to rely on the statements.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The generalization of our results beyond ML debugging requires further assessment. While we motivate our experiment design as a problem-solving task where users can contribute their own ideas and alongside LLM advice, variations between collaboration styles across different tasks can alter the outcomes. We also recruited a specific group of participants who are young university students, which limits the understanding of how sycophancy can affect other population segments. Our computational evaluations are also constrained to the task and do not offer generalized results on how the system prompting may have affected the LLMs.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We investigate the effect of sycophancy on novice-LLM interactions in open-ended problem-solving. We find that sycophancy can reinforce existing misconceptions in users and drive them into over-relying on the LLM. However, most users indicated that they did not notice the characteristics of sycophancy, suggesting that it can be difficult for end users to enact mitigation strategies.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.acknowledgements1" class="ltx_acknowledgements">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_acknowledgements">Acknowledgements.</h6><span id="S6.acknowledgements1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
We thank Robert Soden for his helpful feedback on the qualitative analysis methods and the early draft of the paper.
</span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="bib" class="ltx_bibliography">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_bibliography" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">References</h2>

<ul class="ltx_biblist">
      
<li id="bib.bib2" class="ltx_bibitem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_role_refnum ltx_tag_bibitem"><span id="bib.bib2.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">cbs (2025)</span></span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock"><span id="bib.bib2.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
2025.
</span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock"><span id="bib.bib2.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">AI users form relationships with technology.
